From k149. Past 2 MiB, the supervisor keeps the first and last MiB of a run_* output and drops the middle. The k149 reply cap then cuts the oldest bytes of what is left, and its [... N bytes omitted; a reply holds at most 96 KiB] count leaves the dropped middle out. With 10,000-byte lines and 3 MiB of output, the marker said 1,998,942 bytes, about 1 MiB less than was really cut, and no gap line survives. k149's tool description warns about this. Done when the cap marker's count includes the supervisor's dropped bytes, or a separate line says a middle was dropped, with a test. Lane: unreagent-worker; after k149 lands.

