Choice: an exact count, not "at least N". The dropped byte count was not exposed, so CommandResult gets an unexported dropped field (set in headTailBuffer.result) and Gap(n) (index, dropped int, ok bool) in internal/supervisor/ringbuffer.go: the index of the "[... N bytes omitted]" line among the last n lines of Output, counted the way LinesBefore counts. commandReply hands it to capReply(head, lines, gap, dropped); logs passes -1, 0. When the cut reaches that line, the whole line goes, because a cut inside it would leave a fragment that names no count. The count then adds N and subtracts the line text and the two line breaks String() inserts around it. So the count is of the output own bytes. The marker is sized for size+N, so the reply stays within the cap. In the one case where the cut stops short of that line (the output ends in nearly 1 MiB of blank lines), the line survives and shows the middle itself.

