Why. The content is a 2,900 KiB line, then 300 short lines. The last MiB holds the long line's end plus the 300, so keptLines is 301. n=300 < keptLines, so Window leaves the window as it is (it widens only for keptLines <= n < keptLines+2). The 300 short lines are the window, and LinesBefore gives r.lines - n = 301 - 300 = 1: the long line, the one real line above them. That is right. The reply fits the cap, so there is no byte marker. The reply is exactly "exit 0", a blank line, "[... 1 line omitted]", then the 300 short lines whole: 8 + 21 + 2,999 = 3,028 bytes.

omittedMarker writes the singular for one ("1 line omitted"), but the probe's regexes take only the plural: probeLineRE in zz_probe_k175_test.go:14 and probeLineRE2 in zz_probe_k175c_test.go:13, both (\d+) lines omitted. So the probe read N=0 and counted the 21-byte marker line as body: body (3020) is not a suffix, N=0 want 1. Its expectK (N=1, kept 301) was right. This is the only section-2 case where exactly one line lies above the window: at 297 to 299 the window reaches back to the long line (N=0), and at 301 and 302, N is 2 and 3, plural.

